1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:13,709
In this video you will learn to run a protein BLAST search at N C B I, read the hits table, judge a match by its E value and percent identity, and open an alignment. All you need is a protein sequence.

2
00:00:14,509 --> 00:00:24,876
This is the N C B I protein BLAST page. The blast P tab is already selected, which means a protein query searched against a protein database.

3
00:00:29,026 --> 00:00:42,625
Click the query box and paste your protein sequence. Here we use human hemoglobin beta, UniProt P68871. You can also enter an accession number or a FASTA record.

4
00:00:44,375 --> 00:00:54,935
Next, choose the database to search. We pick UniProt K B Swiss Prot, a smaller, expert curated set, so the hits are well annotated and the search is fast.

5
00:00:56,685 --> 00:01:00,656
Leave the algorithm on blast P, and click the blue BLAST button.

6
00:01:02,406 --> 00:01:10,963
BLAST now compares your sequence against every protein in the database. This usually takes under a minute; the page refreshes by itself.

7
00:01:15,113 --> 00:01:25,875
When the search finishes you land on the results page. The top shows a summary of your job, including the database and your query length, 147 amino acids.

8
00:01:27,625 --> 00:01:34,938
Below is the Descriptions table. Each row is a database protein that matches your query, sorted from best to worst.

9
00:01:36,688 --> 00:01:45,429
The first hit is human hemoglobin beta itself, with a query cover of 100 percent. That is the sanity check that your search worked.

10
00:01:47,179 --> 00:02:03,904
The E value is the number of hits this good you would expect by pure chance in a database this size. Smaller is better. Anything below about 1 e minus 5 is usually a real relationship, and 6 e minus 106 is essentially zero.

11
00:02:05,654 --> 00:02:20,117
Percent identity is the share of aligned positions with the same amino acid. 100 percent is an identical sequence; gorilla is 99 percent and gibbon 98 percent, because these are close relatives.

12
00:02:21,867 --> 00:02:36,947
Further down, identity drops as the species get more distant. Other mammals are around 90 percent identical, and the E values are still tiny, near 1 e minus 90. These are true beta globins from other animals.

13
00:02:41,097 --> 00:02:47,684
To see how a hit lines up with your query, click its description. Let's open the gorilla hit, the second row.

14
00:02:49,434 --> 00:03:02,342
Each alignment shows your query on top and the database protein below. The middle line shows a letter where they are identical, a plus sign where the amino acids are similar, and a blank where they differ.

15
00:03:04,092 --> 00:03:23,985
The header line sums it up. 146 of 147 positions are identical, so 99 percent identity, with no gaps. The single difference is at position 104, where human has arginine and gorilla has lysine, a similar amino acid, shown by the plus sign.

16
00:03:28,135 --> 00:03:38,396
Now let's repeat the search with a different protein, human insulin, UniProt P01308. Back on a fresh search page, paste the sequence.

17
00:03:40,146 --> 00:03:45,197
Choose the same database, Swiss Prot, so the two results are comparable, and click BLAST.

18
00:03:48,928 --> 00:03:52,361
Again, wait while BLAST compares insulin with the database.

19
00:03:54,111 --> 00:04:02,180
Human insulin is again the top hit, at 100 percent identity. That always happens when your protein is in the database.

20
00:04:03,930 --> 00:04:20,976
Now compare with hemoglobin. Apes are still above 97 percent, but the rat is already down to 83 percent. Insulin's precursor contains a signal peptide and a C peptide that change quickly in evolution, so identity falls off much sooner.

21
00:04:22,726 --> 00:04:46,848
Further down, fish and bird insulins are only 45 to 55 percent identical and cover about three quarters of the query. Their E values, around 1 e minus 15 to 1 e minus 20, are larger than hemoglobin's, yet still far below 1 e minus 5, so these are real insulins. Judge a hit by its E value first, then by identity and coverage.

22
00:04:48,498 --> 00:05:04,083
To recap. Paste your sequence, pick a database, and click BLAST. In the hits table, check the E value first, small means the match is not by chance, then percent identity and query cover. Click a description to see the alignment itself.
